{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4adf3761a3ed44009ea965f345a5f609","ordinal":281,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.40","text":"\n\nIntelligence varies a great deal, almost infinitely, in degree. So we say that someone more intelligent may come along than this man whom we call more intelligent than all who have been and are. We are asked to trust the man now said to be more sensible than all who are and have been, because of his intelligence. In the same way we ought rather to trust the man who comes after him and is more intelligent than he is. And when that one has come, we must expect yet another to come who is more intelligent than he is, and after that one another, and so on to infinity."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7b152b691bcf4e248b883678fc6e2d53","ordinal":282,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.41","text":"\n\nAnd it is non-evident whether these people will agree with one another or say conflicting things. So suppose someone is agreed to be more intelligent than all who have been and are. We cannot affirm firmly that no one will be sharper than he is, since that is non-evident. So we will always have to wait for the judgment of the more intelligent person still to come, and never assent to the superior one."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c127642a16e84a5eb9f62035f9a8c8ec","ordinal":283,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.42","text":"\n\nAnd even if we grant, for the sake of argument, that no one is, was or will be more intelligent than the intelligent man we are supposing, it is still not appropriate to trust him. Intelligent people especially like, when they build up a case, to champion unsound positions and make them seem sound and true. So whenever this sharp-witted man says something, we will not know whether he is saying it as the matter is by nature, or presenting what is false as true and persuading us to think of it as true. After all, he is more intelligent than everyone, and for that reason we cannot refute him. So we will not assent to him either as someone who judges things truly. It is possible that he speaks the truth, but we would also suspect that his surpassing sharpness of wit leads him to say what he says because he wants to present falsehoods as true. For these reasons, then, we must not trust in judging things even the man who seems the sharpest of all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9b2c56a8b5ed4ca382dd99cf95b79802","ordinal":284,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.43","text":"\n\nIf someone says that we must attend to the agreement of the many, we will say that this is futile. First, truth is perhaps rare, and for this reason one person may be more sensible than the many. Second, more people disagree with any criterion than agree on it. Those who adopt any criterion other than the one some people seem to agree on disagree with it, and they far outnumber those who agree on it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-894e502affd14f0893ccb6a2c4a94f75","ordinal":285,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.44","text":"\n\nBesides this, those who agree are either in different conditions or in one. As far as the point at issue goes, they are not in different conditions at all, for how would they then say the same things about it? If they are in one condition, the problem is this. The single person who says something different has one condition, and all these who agree also have one. So as far as the conditions we attend to go, no difference is found even in number."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b891e6f470e4416ca8d50cd9b623715d","ordinal":286,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.45","text":"\n\nSo we should not attend to the many rather than to the one. Besides, as we noted in the fourth mode of skepticism, the difference in judgments according to number is inapprehensible. Individual human beings are infinitely many, and we cannot survey the judgments of all of them and declare what the majority of all people assert and what the minority assert. On this ground too, then, preferring judges by their number is absurd."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f94887dc63f1454ab0f3427f4ebf00f2","ordinal":287,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.46","text":"\n\nAnd if we are not to attend to number either, we will not find anyone by whom things will be judged, even though we have granted so much for the sake of argument. So from all this the criterion by which things will be judged is found to be inapprehensible."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-23b94fd89dfd4d43b460b2c35fb9ed31","ordinal":288,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.47","text":"\n\nThe other criteria are cancelled along with this one, since each of them is either a part, an affection or an activity of the human being. So it would perhaps be consistent to move our argument on to one of the next topics, on the grounds that enough has been said about those criteria as well in what we have said here. But so that we do not seem to be avoiding a specific rebuttal of each, we will say a little about them too, as a bonus. First we will discuss the so-called criterion “through which.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4844585c4b5847d0a1472ed2f8e85096","ordinal":289,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.48","text":"\n\nThere has been great and almost endless disagreement about this among the dogmatists. But again we take care to be methodical. On their view the human being is that by which things are judged. And he would have nothing through which he could judge except perception and the intellect, as they themselves also agree. So if we show that he can judge neither through perception alone, nor through the intellect alone, nor through both together, we will have answered all the particular views concisely, for all of them seem to come down to these three positions."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1dc2b53b4da64e94a52d53b14e56c3fd","ordinal":290,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.49","text":"\n\nLet us begin with the senses. Some say that the senses are affected emptily, since nothing real underlies what they seem to perceive. Others say that everything by which they think the senses are moved really exists. Still others say that some of these things exist and some do not. So we will have nothing to assent to. We will not decide the disagreement by perception, since it is perception we are investigating, whether it is affected emptily or truly apprehends. Nor will we decide it by anything else, since on the hypothesis before us there is no other criterion through which one must judge."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-05d67195cfe34ef29f372728f196e2dd","ordinal":291,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.50","text":"\n\nSo it will be undecidable and inapprehensible whether perception is affected emptily or apprehends something. And with this comes the conclusion that we should not attend to perception alone in judging things, since we cannot say whether it apprehends anything in the first place."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ef1eaba2a479451c9860467cf5b752fe","ordinal":292,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.51","text":"\n\nBut let it be granted, for the sake of argument, that the senses are capable of grasping things. Even so, they will be found no less untrustworthy for judging external real objects. At any rate, the senses are moved in contrary ways by external things. Taste, for instance, finds the same honey bitter at one time and sweet at another. To sight the same color ⟨seems⟩ blood-red at one time and white at another. Nor does smell agree with itself."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3d88c6a8e14a458d807eaa0cda569d8e","ordinal":293,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.52","text":"\n\nSomeone with a headache says that perfume is unpleasant, while someone not in that state says it is pleasant. And people possessed by a god, and the delirious, think they hear certain people talking to them whom we do not hear. And the same water seems unpleasant to people with an inflammation because it is excessively hot, but lukewarm to others."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8c1987b50a5e4c9fa41ac8a8f7a849fa","ordinal":294,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.53","text":"\n\nShould one say, then, that all impressions are true, or that these are true and those false, or that all are false? It is impossible to say. We have no agreed criterion through which to judge what we are going to prefer, and we have no true and judged proof available either. The criterion of truth, through which even a true proof ought to be decided, is still under investigation."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-21ec7b78b19641e486c089af9ff49809","ordinal":295,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.54","text":"\n\nFor these reasons, anyone who asks us to trust those in a natural condition [in these matters] but not to trust at all those in an unnatural condition will be talking absurdly. If he says this without proof he will not be believed, and for the reasons already given he will not have a true and judged proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-754280a6014d4700a5248e9413551062","ordinal":296,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.55","text":"\n\nEven if someone were to concede that the impressions of those in a natural condition are trustworthy and those of people in an unnatural condition untrustworthy, judging external real objects through the senses alone will still be found impossible. Sight, even in its natural condition, says the ⟨same⟩ tower is round at one time and square at another. Taste says the same food is unpleasant to people who are full and pleasant to people who are hungry. Hearing likewise perceives the same sound as loud at night and faint by day,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-617428cf24d34015ba14dd5f09f68997","ordinal":297,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.56","text":"\n\nand smell finds the same things foul to most people but not at all to tanners. The same touch is warmed by the entrance hall when we go into the bathhouse, but chilled by it when we come out. So the senses conflict with themselves even in their natural condition. The disagreement is undecidable, since we have no agreed ⟨criterion⟩ through which they can be judged. So the same puzzles must follow. Many other points can also be carried over from what we said earlier about the modes of suspension to establish this. So it would perhaps not be true that perception alone can judge external real objects."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9fbf3f4550c646149dc398d4391ac021","ordinal":298,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.57","text":"\n\nLet our argument move on, then, to the intellect. Those who think we should attend to the intellect alone in judging things will not, first of all, be able to show that the existence of the intellect is apprehensible. Gorgias, in saying that nothing exists, says that the intellect does not exist either, while some assert that it does. How, then, will they decide the disagreement? Not by the intellect, since they would be taking the point at issue for granted. Nor by anything else, since on the hypothesis now before us they say there is nothing else through which things are judged. So it will be undecidable and inapprehensible whether the intellect exists or not. And with this comes the conclusion that we should not attend to the intellect alone in judging things, since it has not yet been apprehended.\n〔1〕 Gorgias of Leontini (fifth century BC), the famous orator, argued in his treatise On What Is Not that nothing exists."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5d8057cb227a4f5b93d3957fb843698c","ordinal":299,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.58","text":"\n\nBut let the intellect be apprehended, and let it be agreed by hypothesis that it exists. I say that it cannot judge things. It does not even see itself precisely, and it disagrees about its own substance, the manner of its coming into being, and the place it is in. So how could it apprehend any of the other things precisely?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-839f2c05ea2a4aa48211bdb4f30f9977","ordinal":300,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.59","text":"\n\nAnd even granting that the intellect is capable of judging things, we will not find out how to judge by it. Intellects differ a great deal. Gorgias’ intellect, by which he says that nothing exists, is one thing. Heraclitus’, by which he says that everything exists, is another. The intellect of those who say that some things exist and some do not is another again. So we will not be able to decide the difference between intellects, nor to say that we ought to follow this man’s intellect but not that man’s at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6e0b76e73f154474a5ecf0ef23dbc297","ordinal":301,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.60","text":"\n\nIf we dare to judge by some particular intellect, we will be assenting to one side of the disagreement and taking the point at issue for granted. If we judge by something else, we will be lying when we say that things must be judged by the intellect alone."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-671bdb5c0476403788cf20b6edff7173","ordinal":302,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.61","text":"\n\nFurther, from what was said about the criterion “by which” [said], we will be able to show several things. We cannot find the intellect that is sharper than the others. And if we do find an intellect sharper than all those that have been and are, we should not attend to it, since it is non-evident whether yet another, sharper than this one, will come along."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f4c9f85fae3d44fdb63e9df26c63b455","ordinal":303,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.62","text":"\n\nAnd even if we posit an intellect than which there could be none more acute, we will not assent to the one who judges through it. We would be wary that he might put forward some false argument and, because he possesses the keenest intellect, be able to persuade us that it is true. So things should not be judged by the intellect alone either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e3c458a23609403f9a5219470f606b9e","ordinal":304,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.63","text":"\n\nIt remains to say that things are judged through both. That again is impossible. Not only do the senses not guide the intellect toward apprehension; they even oppose it. At any rate, from the fact that honey appears bitter to some and sweet to others, Democritus said that it is neither sweet nor bitter, and Heraclitus that it is both. The same argument holds for the other senses and their objects. So the intellect, starting from the senses, is compelled to make different and conflicting assertions, and this is foreign to an apprehensive criterion. Then there is this to be said as well."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2ada7d1b82694bf3ac95a908d45c3d61","ordinal":305,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.64","text":"\n\nThey will judge things either by all the senses and everyone’s intellects, or by some. If someone says by all, he will be asking for the impossible, since so much conflict is apparent in the senses and the intellects. Besides, the verdict of Gorgias’ intellect is that one should attend neither to perception nor to intellect, so the argument will turn itself around. If by some, how will they judge that one should attend to these senses and ⟨this⟩ intellect and not to those? They have no agreed criterion through which to decide between the different senses and intellects."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a1514eebbb3f46888ee221d2e130d9b5","ordinal":306,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.65","text":"\n\nIf they say that we will judge the senses and the intellects by the intellect and the senses, ⟨..........⟩ but not by some, they are taking the point at issue for granted. For what we are investigating is whether anyone can judge through these."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-aa7b290eb768421ba222dcef5b01a0d1","ordinal":307,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.66","text":"\n\nThen there is this to be said as well. They will judge in one of four ways. They will judge both the senses and the intellects by the senses. Or they will judge both the senses and the intellects by the intellects. Or they will judge the senses by the senses and the intellects by the intellects. Or they will judge the intellects by the senses and the senses by the intellect. If they want to judge both by the senses or both by the intellect, they will no longer be judging through perception and intellect, but through one of these, whichever they choose. And the puzzles stated earlier will dog them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e919bc29fdda4fcdae45f8fb0b1cdd7a","ordinal":308,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.67","text":"\n\nSuppose they decide the senses by the senses and the intellects by the intellect. Senses conflict with senses and intellects with intellects. So whichever of the conflicting senses they adopt for judging the other senses, they will be taking the point at issue for granted. They will be treating one part of the disagreement as already credible in order to decide things that are just as much in question as it is."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ab4a3660d09b480e924d1de82fe16d6e","ordinal":309,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.68","text":"\n\nThe same argument holds for the intellects too. Suppose instead they decide the intellects by the senses and the senses by the intellect. Then we find the mode of reciprocity. For the senses to be decided, the intellects must have been judged first. For the intellects to be tested, the senses must have been thoroughly sorted out first."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-078491f21bce435db701b2c18d42501d","ordinal":310,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.69","text":"\n\nCriteria of one kind cannot be decided by criteria of the same kind, nor both kinds by one kind, nor each kind by the other kind in turn. So we will not be able to prefer one intellect to another or one perception to another. For this reason we will have nothing through which to judge. We cannot judge by all the senses and intellects, and we will not know which ones we should judge by and which not. So we will have nothing through which to judge things. For these reasons too, then, the criterion “through which” would be unreal."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fd5e5600c4d84f4db8bdb76eee99ebf7","ordinal":311,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.70","text":"\n\nNext, then, let us look at the criterion according to which they say things are judged. The first thing that can be said about it is that ⟨......⟩ it is inconceivable. They say that an impression is an imprinting in the ruling part. Now the soul and the ruling part are breath, or something finer than breath, as they say. So no one will be able to conceive an imprinting in it either by depression and projection, as we see with seals, or by the fabled “alteration” kind. It would not retain the memory of the many theorems that make up an art, since the earlier ones would be wiped out by each alteration that came after.\n〔1〕 Cleanthes took the imprint literally, like a seal in wax, while Chrysippus called it an “alteration.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a2cb4d01fae6477b9a6196dfd0bfccdd","ordinal":312,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.71","text":"\n\nEven if the impression could be conceived, it will be inapprehensible. It is an affection of the ruling part, and the ruling part is not apprehended, as we showed. So we will not apprehend its affection either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bc8183f11c6240a8b1b36a7391dc94a5","ordinal":313,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.72","text":"\n\nThen, even if we were to grant that the impression is apprehended, things cannot be judged according to it. The intellect does not make contact with external things through itself, nor ⟨through itself⟩ receive impressions, as they say; it does so through the senses. The senses do not apprehend external real objects, but only, if anything, their own affections. So the impression too will be of the sense’s affection, and that differs from the external real object. Honey is not the same as my tasting sweetness, nor wormwood the same as my tasting bitterness; they are different."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-13255832ff0c4c6985b09a2a52cbd2a8","ordinal":314,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.73","text":"\n\nIf this affection differs from the external real object, the impression will not be of the external real object but of something else, different from it. So if the intellect judges according to this impression, it judges badly and not according to the real object. So it is absurd to say that external things are judged according to the impression."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-57936c3661694bdb8a2eaa43844a4a07","ordinal":315,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.74","text":"\n\nNor can one say that the soul apprehends external real objects through the affections of the senses because those affections are similar to the external real objects. How will the intellect know whether the affections of the senses are similar to the objects of sense? It does not itself encounter external things. And the senses do not reveal those things’ nature to it, only their own affections, as I argued from the modes of suspension."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9bb29b26075b4b8cbcf1c23e07056903","ordinal":316,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.75","text":"\n\nTake someone who doesn’t know Socrates but has looked at a picture of him. He doesn’t know whether the picture resembles Socrates. In the same way the intellect observes how the senses are affected but does not see the external things, so it will not know whether the senses’ affections resemble the external objects either. So it will not be able to judge these things by the impression on the basis of resemblance either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9fd3224965f94a53830cfc5edb4cfffd","ordinal":317,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.76","text":"\n\nBut let us concede, for the sake of argument, that besides being conceivable and apprehended, the impression is also the kind of thing by which objects can be judged, even though the argument has pointed to the very opposite. Then we will trust either every impression ⟨or some. But if every one, clearly we will also trust Xeniades’ impression⟩, according to which he said that all impressions are untrustworthy. The argument will then turn itself around: not all impressions are such that objects can be judged by them.\n〔1〕 Xeniades of Corinth (fifth century BC) held that every impression and opinion is false."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b610b43c3aca4560abdcbf73582a73cb","ordinal":318,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.77","text":"\n\nIf some, how will we decide that it is right to trust these impressions and to distrust those? If we decide without an impression, they will be granting that the impression is superfluous for judging, since they will be saying that some objects can be judged without it. If with an impression, how will they get hold of the impression they bring in to judge the other impressions?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6e0660ea801340bb819236b463f64ce8","ordinal":319,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.78","text":"\n\nThey will need yet another impression to judge that other impression, and another to judge that one, and so on to infinity. But it is impossible to decide infinitely many things. So it is impossible to find out which impressions we should use as criteria and which not at all. Even if we grant that objects should be judged by impressions, then, the argument turns itself around either way, both if we trust all of them and if we trust some as criteria and distrust others. It follows that impressions should not be adopted as criteria for judging objects."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9ca5416237cd4a6a853775ef79304551","ordinal":320,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.79","text":"\n\nFor now it is enough to have said this much, in outline, against the criterion according to which objects were said to be judged. But you should be aware that it is not our purpose to declare that the criterion of truth is unreal; that would be dogmatic. The dogmatists seem to have established plausibly that there is a criterion of truth, so we have set against them arguments that seem plausible. We do not affirm that these are true, or that they are more plausible than their opposites. We conclude to suspension of judgment because of the apparent equal plausibility of these arguments and of those put forward by the dogmatists."}]}